/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  membrane27_swro_energy_recovery / system/solverDict

  ONE recycle, ONE tear: the brine the RO stage produces LAST is what the
  exchanger consumes SECOND, so `brine` is cut and seeded in 0/brine.

  WHY WEGSTEIN AND NOT THE DEFAULT NEWTON.  The loop's gain is close to one
  by construction: the exchanger returns (1 - leakage) = 98.5 % of the brine
  VOLUME to the membrane feed, so the fixed point
      Q_RO = Q_HPpump + 0.985 (Q_RO - Q_permeate)
  is set by the 1.5 % that leaks and by how the permeate moves with the
  feed -- the plant's recovery is a knife edge on the high-pressure pump's
  flow, which is exactly why a real SWRO train controls that pump to the
  permeate it wants.  A Newton step on such a loop divides a small
  nonlinearity by that 1.5 % and overshoots to an unphysical trial (a brine
  with no water, a booster asked to pump DOWN) that the units refuse; the
  Wegstein secant stays on physical trials.  The q bound is widened from
  its default floor (-1) so the secant may take its full step on a loop
  whose slope is 0.985 (the default floor would leave a 0.97 contraction
  per iteration); both solvers converge to the same unique fixed point.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recycleSolver          Wegstein;
recycleWegsteinQmin    -100;
recycleWegsteinQmax    0;
recycleMaxIter         200;
recycleTol             1.0e-6;
